WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.640
The A to Z of isms... Libertarianism.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.200
ů the words of the then US President
Ronald Reagan back in 1986.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.200
Reagan's mockery of the
very government he headed

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.560
tapped into widespread American
support for a political philosophy

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.760
that grew throughout
the 20th Century.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.280
Its most vocal champions
such as Senator Rand Paul

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.040
and the Koch brothers,
are on the political fringes.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.600
But libertarian thinking has
influenced mainstream politics,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.720
from Ronald Reagan's
attacks on government

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.440
to Margaret Thatcher's desire to...

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.240
Libertarianism's roots
go back much further though.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.000
As their name suggests,
libertarians see their philosophy

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.800
as embodying the principles
of individual liberty,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.880
promoted by the
European Enlightenment

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.640
and American independence
in the 18th Century.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.480
Political libertarianism is
particularly at home in the USA,

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.320
which celebrates the pioneer values

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.000
of self-reliance and
rugged individualism.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.400
Key among these
is the principle of self-ownership.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.560
Your life belongs to you
and no-one else.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.000
Hence, the liberty they defend

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.120
is what philosopher
Isaiah Berlin called...

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.800
ů especially governments.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.400
Libertarians regard all government
as a necessary evil.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.080
They favour a minimal state,
which the leading libertarian

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.360
philosopher Robert Nozick said,
should be...

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.360
Such a minimal state would not
even provide public services

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.080
such as health and education.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.360
For libertarians,
welfare states create dependency

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.720
and stifle initiative.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.640
These principles make libertarians
very critical of taxation.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.320
Nozick went so far as to say that...

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.840
Although libertarians tend
to be politically right-wing,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.040
there is also a fairly strong
tradition of left libertarianism,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.720
such as that advocated by Canadian
philosopher Hillel Steiner.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.440
He claims that no-one has a right to
claim ownership of common goods,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.440
such as land.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.280
With an equal distribution
of natural resources for everyone

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.240
there would be sufficient equality,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.920
while leaving people
as free as possible

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.320
to decide how to live
their own lives.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.560
Critics of libertarianism argue
that we are not truly free

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.280
unless we have access to things
like decent housing,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.480
healthcare and education.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.040
But we could only have this positive
freedom, as Berlin called it,

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.960
in a state that provides services
and redistributes some wealth.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.280
Libertarianism may not be
well-known as a philosophy,

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.880
but its spirit is invoked every time
people attack "big government"

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.600
or "the nanny state".

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.360
It's probably the most
influential political philosophy

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.640
most people have never heard of.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.040
Thanks for watching. 
